{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_dnqi6duj7x7bv7iz6p2xkvxesju3cz465z5lk4p3zf4aa54mxuwa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The C-to-Rust translation of the GFC (Generalized Feistel Cipher) project is complete:\n\n**Summary of implementation:**\n- **`ror`/`rol`**: 32-bit rotate right/left bitwise operations\n- **`round`**: Speck round function — `(x ↦ ror(x,8)+y ⊕ k, y ↦ rol(y,3) ⊕ x)`\n- **`speck_expand`**: Generates 27 round keys from a 4-word key, mirroring the C implementation's use of array `a[3]` and accumulator `b`\n- **`speck_encrypt`**: Applies 27 Speck rounds to a 2-word plaintext (note: C uses `R(ct[1], ct[0], K[i])` so I split the slice to mutate both halves)\n- **`GFC::new`**: Computes `a = b = ⌈√range⌉` and precomputes all `r * 27` Speck round keys, treating each `uint64_t` key word as little-endian split into two `u32`s (matching how the C cast works on little-endian platforms)\n- **`f`**: Applies Speck to a 64-bit input split into `[low, high]` u32s, returns the joined u64 result\n- **`fe`/`fe_inv`**: Feistel rounds matching the paper (Black & Rogaway, 2002)\n- **`encrypt`/`decrypt`**: Cycle-walking — repeat `fe`/`fe_inv` until output `< M`\n\nAll 8 test cases from `gfc_test.c` pass, including edge cases (range=0, range=1, rounds=0). No `unsafe` code, no FFI calls — pure idiomatic Rust."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":254,"cache_read_input_tokens":30574,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":254,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb46edcb-f054-4056-bca2-c6c23cb3dfc3","uuid":"b935325b-bc9a-4689-9ca1-ebe532b6d9b7"}
